      "reason": "summary-or-gloss-unfaithful",
      "note": "Parts a and b name the text by a level term that the statement does not define: it does not say whose level or which level is meant. The Summary turns it into text at the child's level, and Limits states it outright as the child's own reading level (In a child's hands repeats it). That is a condition the statement does not state; the term can as well mean the level of the grade, which is how the neighbouring RL.1.10 and RI.1.10 set text difficulty. A designer would take from the record that text difficulty follows the individual child. Keep the term neutral, or say in Limits that the statement leaves the level undefined. The rest of the Summary and Limits is faithful (accuracy, rate and expression on repeated oral readings; context used to confirm or self-correct; rereading only as needed). Wording and code check out: the opening line and parts a to c are present, in order, with nothing else in the cell and no boldface.",
